    "# ISE 201 — Mini Project 3\n",
    "## Can We Trust the Ride Data? Linear algebra for a bike-share operator\n",
    "\n",
    "**Dr. Shilpa Gupta · ISE @ SJSU · Fall 2026**\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### The situation\n",
    "\n",
    "Potomac Mobility Partners is a small analytics consultancy. Your client is the operations office of a city bike-share program. You have two years of real daily ride counts and weather from Washington, DC (2011–2012).\n",
    "\n",
    "Renata Alvarez, the client's Director of Operations, has three problems on her desk:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Crew planning.** Her rebalancing crews are scheduled by gut feel. She wants a demand model she can explain to the city council, which means coefficients that *mean something*.\n",
    "2. **Data she can trust.** Her fleet lead, Marcus Bell, suspects some of the weather-station records are wrong. Nobody has checked.\n",
    "3. **Maintenance windows.** Bikes get serviced on low-demand days, and bad weather means low demand. She wants to know how long bad-weather spells typically last.\n",
    "\n",
    "Every tool you need is in the **Week 10 companion notebook**. This project is the same toolkit on a new problem. Each part below tells you exactly where to look.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Reference map\n",
    "\n",
    "| Part | Client question | Week 10 notebook reference |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| 1 | What does a year of operations look like as one matrix? | Part B Step 1 · Part A §A3 |\n",
    "| 2 | How many extra rides does a warmer day bring? | Part B Step 2 · Part A §A1 |\n",
    "| 3 | Which columns are redundant, and which can't be used at all? | Part B Steps 3–4, 6 · Part A §A2, §A6 |\n",
    "| 4 | A demand model Renata can explain | Part B Steps 4–5 · Part A §A5 |\n",
    "| 5 | Which weather records are wrong? | Part B Step 7 · Part A §A7 |\n",
    "| 6 | How long do bad-weather spells last? | Part B Step 8 · Part A §A5, §A6 |\n",
    "| 7 | Memo to Renata | Wrap-up table |\n",
    "\n",
    "### Your version of the data\n",
    "Each student analyzes a different **12-month window** of the two years, selected from your SIS User ID. Your numbers will differ from your classmates', so compare *methods*, not answers. A notebook run with someone else's ID receives no credit.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Grading (20 points)\n",
    "\n",
    "| Part | Points | Full credit means |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| 1. Data matrix | 2 | $\\bar{\\mathbf{x}}$, $\\mathbf{S}$, $\\mathbf{R}$ built with matrix products and checked |\n",
    "| 2. Temperature signal | 2 | Angle, correlation and slope computed and interpreted in rides per °C |\n",
    "| 3. Redundancy audit | 3 | Exact vs. near dependency identified with the right tool for each; leakage explained |\n",
    "| 4. Demand model | 4 | QR fit; coefficient instability shown and explained; interpretation vs. prediction distinguished |\n",
    "| 5. Data quality audit | 4 | Mahalanobis via $\\mathbf{S}^{-1/2}$; top records investigated; a justified keep / fix / drop decision; refit |\n",
    "| 6. Weather spells | 3 | $\\mathbf{P}$ estimated by counting; stationary distribution from an eigenvector; expected spell lengths from a linear solve; sample-size caveat |\n",
    "| 7. Memo | 2 | 150–200 words, numbers from *your* window, a decision for each of Renata's three problems, one specific limitation |\n",
    "\n",
    "**AI use:** allowed and encouraged under the course policy. In the disclosure cell at the end, name the tool, summarize your prompts, and say what you verified or changed. Undisclosed AI use is treated as an integrity issue.\n",
    "\n",
    "AI/tool-use note: This notebook was AI-assisted and instructor-verified.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Data source:** Fanaee-T, H. and Gama, J. (2013), *Event labeling combining ensemble detectors and background knowledge*, Progress in AI. UCI Machine Learning Repository, Bike Sharing Dataset (Capital Bikeshare, Washington DC)."
   ]
