   "source": [
    "**3a.** For each pair (casual/registered/cnt, and tempC/atempC), say whether the dependency is **exact** or **near**, and name the quantity that detected it. Read the smallest eigenvector: which combination of columns is nearly constant?\n",
    "\n",
    "**3b.** Marcus proposes predicting tomorrow's `cnt` from tomorrow's `registered` rides, because \"the fit is almost perfect.\" Explain to him why this model is useless for crew planning, even though nothing is wrong with the linear algebra."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "06238ebf",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "06238ebf"
   },
   "source": [
    "> 3a. The ride variables are exactly dependent because cnt = casual + registered every day. That is why the three-column matrix has rank 2 instead of 3 and the combination [1, 1, -1] produces zero. The temperature measures are nearly, not exactly, dependent: the smallest eigenvalue of the standardized weather correlation matrix is 0.0035, and its eigenvector nearly subtracts standardized tempC from standardized atempC. The near cancellation indicates that the two columns carry almost the same information, but not perfectly, so the rank test alone would not identify their redundancy.\n",
    ">\n",
    "> 3b. I would not use tomorrow's registered rides to predict tomorrow's total rides for advance crew scheduling. Registered rides are part of the total by definition, and their value is only known after the riding happens. Using them would make a model look impressively accurate on paper while leaking information Renata cannot have when deciding staffing."
   ]
  },
