   "source": [
    "**4a.** What happened to the tempC coefficient when atempC was added? Explain the change using what you found in Part 3.2.\n",
    "\n",
    "**4b.** On a typical day, Models A and B predict almost the same number of rides. So what is wrong with giving Renata Model B? Compare with the generalized-inverse result in Week 10 Part B Step 4: there, predictions agreed on *every* home. Here they diverge sharply on one day. Which day, and what is special about it? (Keep that date in mind for Part 5.)\n",
    "\n",
    "**4c.** Which model do you recommend, and what does its temperature coefficient mean in plain words?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2d01c413",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "2d01c413"
   },
   "source": [
    "> 4a. Model A assigns tempC about 114.10 rides per °C; in Model B, its coefficient falls to 56.42, while atempC gets 53.61. Since tempC and atempC are correlated at about 0.996, Model B struggles to separate their individual effects. Each coefficient now represents changing one temperature measure while holding the other almost-identical measure fixed—an unusual situation that makes either number hard to explain.\n",
    ">\n",
    "> 4b. Model B raises R² only from 0.5691 to 0.5700, and its prediction differs from Model A by a median of just 23 rides. But on July 21, 2011, the predictions differ by 189 rides. That was an extreme-heat day when apparent temperature was especially high relative to measured air temperature. The models disagree there because the tiny differences between normally similar temperature columns become important at an unusual combination of values. This is different from Week 10's exact-redundancy example: an exact identity can give different coefficient vectors with identical fitted predictions, whereas these columns only almost duplicate one another, so the predicted values can actually change. The almost negligible average fit improvement is not worth the added instability.\n",
    ">\n",
    "> 4c. I recommend Model A for an understandable staffing baseline. Holding humidity, wind, and working-day status constant, an extra 1°C in air temperature is associated with about 114 more daily rides. That is a conditional association in historical data, not proof of causation; Renata should combine it with short-term forecasts and operational judgment."
   ]
  },
