   },
   "source": [
    "> 5a. March 10, 2011 has the largest squared Mahalanobis distance, 34.92. Its 0% recorded humidity is an extreme single-variable reading (z = -4.25), and rides were also quite low (z about -2.38). July 21, 2011 is next at 33.61 even though no individual z-score exceeds ±3. Its apparent temperature was 41.32°C while air temperature was 33.42°C. Since these two columns are usually extremely correlated (r = 0.996), a large departure from their usual relationship stands out when the variables are considered jointly. Mahalanobis distance captures those unusual combinations instead of checking each measurement independently.\n",
    ">\n",
    "> 5b. For March 10, I would fix the humidity if an independently verified station reading can be found. Otherwise, I would flag the 0% as suspect and drop that day from the weather-based model fit, while keeping its actual 623 rides in the operations record. The National Weather Service documented heavy rain and flooding in the area around that date, so zero humidity is not believable even though unusually low ridership is. For July 21, I would keep the record: the District activated its extreme-heat response, making unusual heat and apparent temperature plausible as a real event rather than a bad sensor. For crew planning, genuine unusual conditions matter and should be retained; for station maintenance, the March humidity sensor deserves investigation without erasing the real riding outcome. Supporting records: [NWS storm report](https://www.weather.gov/media/lwx/stormdata/2011/storm0311.pdf) and [District government heat advisory](https://dpr.dc.gov/release/district-government-activates-multi-agency-response-extreme-heat-and-humidity-0)."
   ]
  },
