   "source": [
    "**5c.** Did your decision change Model A's coefficients much? Why might a record that is clearly wrong in one column (atempC or humPct) barely move a model that uses only some of the columns?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cde7e922",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "cde7e922"
   },
   "source": [
    "> 5c. Dropping March 10 changes Model A's temperature coefficient from 114.10 to 113.98 and wind from -62.35 to -62.66; humidity changes more, from -16.13 to -18.78, and working-day status moves from -7.00 to +10.55. The main temperature relationship is therefore quite stable, but the other coefficients are not literally identical. This makes sense because Model A uses tempC, humidity, wind, and working-day status, but not atempC. A bad value in an excluded column cannot directly alter its fit; the erroneous humidity value is included, so removing that day matters more for humidity. One anomalous day out of 365 also has limited influence on some coefficients, depending on its predictor pattern."
   ]
