   "source": [
    "**6a.** How close is the eigenvector to the observed share of days? Why should they agree?\n",
    "\n",
    "**6b.** Renata wants to schedule two-day maintenance blocks starting on a rainy day. What do your numbers suggest? How much would you trust the rain row of $P$, given how many rain days it is based on?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5ad36286",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "5ad36286"
   },
   "source": [
    "> 6a. The stationary distribution from the eigenvector is approximately 0.630 clear, 0.332 mist, and 0.038 rain, close to the actual sample shares of 0.627, 0.334, and 0.038. They agree because the transition matrix was calculated from consecutive observations of the same year: over a long run, the proportions flowing into and out of each weather state must nearly balance. They are not exactly equal because this is a finite window with starting and ending days, and a fixed transition matrix ignores changing seasons.\n",
    ">\n",
    "> 6b. Beginning on a rainy day, the estimated mean wait until the next clear day is 2.71 days, compared with 2.19 days when starting from mist. But a mean is not a promise of two uninterrupted low-demand days. Of the 14 rain days with a next-day record, only 4 were followed by another rainy day (0.286), 6 by mist (0.429), and 4 by clear (0.286). So rain may turn into mist, or even clear weather, before the maintenance block ends. I would tentatively reserve lower-demand days but confirm with the forecast and keep crews flexible. Fourteen transitions are a small basis for estimating the rain row, so I would not rely on this probability alone for a costly schedule."
   ]
  },
